   $q$Which evidence best supports the idea that more than one person may have worked on the sampler?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The reverse showed knots, crossings and short tails of thread.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"weak-evidence"},{"text":"The label was typed on a yellowed card in the front hall.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"},{"text":"Visitors admired the tiny alphabet and the rows of numbers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"},{"text":"The two halves of the border show different stitch tension and direction.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The two halves of the border show different stitch tension and direction.$q$, $q$Ms. Achterberg notes that the left half is "tight and even" while the right half is looser and pulled differently, which suggests different hands. Knots and loose tails are what Mei-Lin "expected" on any sampler, so they prove nothing.$q$, 'draft', '0eeda0be-76cb-58e3-b418-43df3bd2282e')
